{"name":"Silver Dusk","hex":"#b4c8d2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lustrous, white, metallic element, atomic number 47, atomic weight 107.87, symbol Ag.","Coins made from silver or any similar white metal."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from silver.","Made from another white metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver"},{"term":"dusk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tending to darkness or blackness; moderately dark or black; dusky."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The time after the sun has set but when the sky is still lit by sunlight; the evening twilight period.","A darkish colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dusk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dusk","blocks":[{"text":"Dusk occurs at the darkest stage of twilight, or at the very end of astronomical twilight after sunset and just before nightfall. At predusk, during early to intermediate stages of twilight, enough light in the sky under clear conditions may occur to read outdoors without artificial illumination; however, at the end of civil twilight, such lighting is required to read outside. The term dusk usually refers to astronomical dusk, or the darkest part of twilight before night begins."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Landers_in_the_Mojave_Desert_at_dusk%2C_looking_west_from_Joshua_Tree%2C_California_USA.jpg/330px-Landers_in_the_Mojave_Desert_at_dusk%2C_looking_west_from_Joshua_Tree%2C_California_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dusk"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"},{"title":"Silver (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver or metallic gray is a color tone resembling gray that is a representation of the color of polished silver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png/330px-Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Scots_pine_at_dusk%2C_Ridley_Plain%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_296737.jpg/330px-Scots_pine_at_dusk%2C_Ridley_Plain%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_296737.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Scots_pine_at_dusk%2C_Ridley_Plain%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_296737.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Scots_pine_at_dusk%2C_Ridley_Plain%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_296737.jpg/330px-Scots_pine_at_dusk%2C_Ridley_Plain%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_296737.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AScots_pine_at_dusk%2C_Ridley_Plain%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_296737.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Dusk_at_the_shore%2C_Silverdale_-_g